Arguments: {"idea": {
  "Name": "strategy_arbitration",
  "Title": "Retrieve or Assemble? How LLMs Arbitrate Between Memorized Shortcuts and Compositional Computation, and Why Choosing Wrong Hides Known Knowledge",
  "Short Hypothesis": "For many derivable facts an LLM has two internal pathways to the answer: a memorized 'shortcut' that recalls the answer atomically, and a 'compositional' pathway that assembles it from intermediate sub-facts. We hypothesize an internal, query-conditioned arbiter selects which pathway drives the output; a distinct and common failure mode is misarbitration \u2014 the model commits to whichever pathway is weaker for that query even though the other pathway holds the correct answer. We predict (1) which pathway is selected is linearly decodable at intermediate layers before the answer is written, (2) misarbitration accounts for a substantial fraction of failures on facts the model can otherwise produce via the alternate pathway, and (3) a lightweight steering intervention that biases the arbiter toward the stronger pathway recovers these hidden answers more selectively than weight editing.",
  "Related Work": "Prior work shows LLMs solve two-hop tasks either compositionally (with detectable intermediate variables) or 'directly'/idiomatically, and that mechanism choice is only weakly correlated with accuracy and tracks embedding-space linearity (How Do LLMs Compose Functions?, 2025); probabilistic recall-and-extract and distributional parallel-reasoning accounts describe internal multi-hop dynamics (Layer-Order Inversion, 2026; Distributional Reasoning, 2024); co-occurrence shortcuts are distinguished from true factual associations by layer (Co-occurrence is not Factual Association, 2024); grokking integrates memorized facts into reasoning circuits (Grokked Transformers, 2024). All of these characterize that two mechanisms exist or describe multi-hop dynamics, but none ask whether the model actively *arbitrates* between an atomic-shortcut pathway and a compositional pathway on a per-query basis, whether that arbitration is predictable/steerable before the answer, or whether *misarbitration* is a recoverable failure mode. My prior proposals concern single-fact routing gates, format-bound duplicate copies, and interference among competing candidate facts; this proposal is orthogonal: it concerns selection between two *procedurally distinct* routes to the same answer.",
  "Abstract": "Large language models can reach the same fact by different internal routes: recalling it as a memorized association, or assembling it from constituent sub-facts. We hypothesize that the model arbitrates between these routes on a per-query basis, and that a common, overlooked failure mode is misarbitration \u2014 committing to whichever route is weaker for the current query even though the alternate route encodes the correct answer. We study this on derivable facts (e.g., 'the capital of the country where X was born') for which both a memorized shortcut and a two-hop compositional path can in principle yield the answer. We first build, per fact, evidence for which routes are viable: probing whether the compositional intermediate (bridge entity) is decodable in the residual stream, and whether the atomic shortcut fires (via co-occurrence-based and single-hop-recall diagnostics). We then test three claims: (1) which route drives the output is linearly decodable from intermediate activations before the answer token is written; (2) using activation patching to isolate the pathways, misarbitration failures \u2014 where the model outputs the wrong answer despite the alternate route being internally viable \u2014 form a substantial and identifiable fraction of errors; and (3) a low-rank steering vector that biases the arbiter toward the viable route recovers correct answers with higher specificity and lower collateral damage than ROME/MEMIT editing. Experiments span GPT-J-6B, Llama-3-8B, and Qwen2.5-7B on SOCRATES, PopQA, and T-REx-derived two-hop sets, evaluating route-prediction AUROC, misarbitration prevalence, recovery rate, specificity, and fluency. Confirming these claims reframes many knowledge failures as strategy-selection errors and yields a cheap, non-destructive way to surface answers the model could compute but does not.",
  "Experiments": [
    "1. Dual-route fact construction: From SOCRATES, PopQA, and T-REx build two-hop derivable facts of the form g(f(x)) (e.g., capital-of-birth-country) where each fact has (a) a compositional route via a bridge entity f(x) and (b) a potential atomic shortcut (direct x->g(f(x)) association). For each model, measure single-hop competence on f(x) and g(z) separately, direct-composition accuracy on g(f(x)), and use co-occurrence diagnostics (does x co-appear with the answer in shortcut-detection prompts) to estimate shortcut viability.",
    "2. Route-viability labeling: For each fact/model, label whether the compositional route is viable (bridge entity f(x) is decodable in the residual stream via logit-lens/tuned-lens at intermediate layers, following SOCRATES-style latent-recall probes) and whether the shortcut route is viable (answer decodable early without bridge signature). This yields per-fact route availability.",
    "3. Route-selection decoding: Train a linear probe on intermediate-layer residual-stream activations (before the answer token is produced) to predict which route drives the eventual output (compositional vs shortcut), using activation-patching-derived ground truth (see #4). Metric: cross-validated AUROC and the earliest layer at which route choice is decodable; label-shuffle control.",
    "4. Pathway isolation via patching: Causally separate the two routes with activation patching \u2014 (a) patch/ablate the bridge-entity representation at the last-subject position to knock out the compositional route and observe whether output survives via shortcut; (b) suppress the early atomic-shortcut signal and observe whether output survives via composition. Use these dissociations to (i) verify each route's causal contribution and (ii) define ground-truth route usage for probe training.",
    "5. Misarbitration prevalence: Define misarbitration failures as cases where the model answers incorrectly yet the alternate route is internally viable (bridge decodable or shortcut decodable per #2/#4). Quantify the fraction of all errors that are misarbitration vs genuine ignorance (neither route viable) across datasets and models; report per-relation breakdowns.",
    "6. Arbiter-steering intervention: Learn a low-rank steering vector as the mean activation difference between correct compositional-route runs and matched misarbitrated failure runs (and symmetrically a shortcut-biasing vector). At inference on held-out misarbitration failures, add the vector biasing toward the viable route at the identified layer. Metrics: recovery rate, specificity (accuracy on 200 unrelated facts pre/post), fluency (perplexity on held-out text), recovered-facts-per-unit-collateral-damage; compare against ROME/MEMIT edits and a random-direction control.",
    "7. Transfer test: Test whether a single arbiter-steering vector generalizes across facts and relation types (train on one relation subset, apply to held-out relations), reporting cross-relation recovery to distinguish a general arbitration signal from fact-specific fixes.",
    "8. Cross-model replication: Repeat core analyses (2-6) on GPT-J-6B, Llama-3-8B, and Qwen2.5-7B to assess architecture-generality."
  ],
  "Risk Factors and Limitations": [
    "Shortcut vs compositional 'routes' may not be cleanly separable when representations are entangled (superposition); patching-based dissociation may leave ambiguous cases, which we report as coverage limits rather than force into the dichotomy.",
    "Logit-lens/tuned-lens decoding of the bridge entity is noisy; we mitigate with tuned-lens, answer-type filtering, and causal patching as the decisive test rather than lens readouts alone.",
    "Route choice may be graded/mixed (both pathways contribute) rather than a discrete arbiter; a graded result would weaken the clean arbitration framing but remains informative, and we report continuous route-contribution measures.",
    "Steering toward a 'viable' route could induce confident hallucination on facts where the route only appears viable; strict specificity and perplexity controls are essential and may cap practical utility.",
    "A single transferable arbiter vector may not exist if arbitration is fact-specific, limiting the intervention's generality though still scientifically informative.",
    "Distinguishing misarbitration from subtle storage weakness is hard; matched-pair design and route-availability controls reduce but may not fully eliminate confounds.",
    "Findings on 6-8B models may not transfer to frontier-scale models beyond an academic budget."
  ]
}}
