Arguments: {"idea": {
"Name": "property_collapse",
"Title": "Property Collapse: When Shared Relational Subspaces Cause LLMs to Retrieve the Right Property but the Wrong Attribute",
"Short Hypothesis": "LLMs organize relational knowledge around coarse-grained shared 'properties' (e.g., a country-of-X subspace serving country-of-capital, country-of-food, country-of-birthplace). We hypothesize a distinct, previously uncharacterized failure mode\u2014property collapse\u2014in which the model correctly retrieves the shared property value but fails to preserve the fine-grained relation-specific distinction, producing a confidently wrong attribute drawn from a sibling relation that shares the property. We predict (1) errors concentrate on relations that share a property with a competing relation, (2) the shared-property subspace is causally responsible (patching it transfers answers across sibling relations while patching the residual relation-specific component does not), and (3) a targeted 'relation-disambiguation' steering vector that sharpens the relation-specific component recovers correct attributes more selectively than weight editing.",
"Related Work": "Hernandez et al. (2023) show relation decoding is often linear (LRE); Chen et al. (2025, 'Structure of Relation Decoding Linear Operators') show these decoders are property-centric\u2014shared across semantically-close relations rather than relation-specific\u2014explaining limited relation-wise generalization. 'On Relation-Specific Neurons' (2025) reports neuron versatility and interference (deactivating one relation's neurons changes another's recall). 'Does Knowledge Localization Hold True?' (2024) shows entity and relation knowledge are stored separately. Non-linear relational composition (2025) shows generalization scales with entity semantic similarity. All of these characterize that relational structure is shared/property-based, but none identify property collapse as a concrete, predictable failure mode of factual answering, none causally decompose retrieval into a shared-property component and a relation-specific component to localize the failure, and none propose sharpening the relation-specific component as a non-destructive fix. This is orthogonal to my prior proposals (expression gating, format-bound copies, candidate competition, route arbitration, pre-retrieval familiarity): here the object is the shared geometry of relation decoders and the relation-confusion errors it produces.",
"Abstract": "Recent interpretability work shows large language models decode relational facts using linear operators that are not relation-specific but property-centric: a single 'country-of-X' subspace, for example, is reused across country-of-capital, country-of-food, and country-of-birthplace. We ask whether this shared organization creates a predictable failure mode. We propose property collapse: when answering a query, the model reliably retrieves the shared coarse property (the right country) but fails to preserve the fine-grained relation-specific distinction, yielding a confident attribute belonging to a sibling relation that shares the property. Under this account, some knowledge failures are not storage or gating failures but binding failures within a collapsed shared subspace. We test three claims. (1) Prevalence: factual errors concentrate on relations that share a property with a competing relation, and the wrong answers are disproportionately valid attributes of a sibling relation for the same subject, above frequency- and similarity-matched baselines. (2) Causal decomposition: using linear relational embeddings we split each relation's decoder into a shared-property component (common across sibling relations) and a relation-specific residual; activation patching shows the shared component transfers answers across siblings while the residual carries the disambiguating signal, and property-collapse errors correspond to a weak relation-specific residual. (3) Intervention: a low-rank relation-disambiguation steering vector that amplifies the residual component recovers correct attributes with higher specificity and less collateral damage than ROME editing. Experiments span GPT-J-6B, Llama-3-8B, and Qwen2.5-7B on LRE relation sets and T-REx/PopQA, evaluating error-type prevalence, causal transfer, recovery rate, specificity, and fluency.",
"Experiments": [
"1. Sibling-relation grouping: Using the LRE relation collection and T-REx/PopQA, group relations by shared coarse property (e.g., country-of-capital, country-of-food, country-of-birthplace all share country-of-X), following the property-centric taxonomy from Chen et al. (2025). Build subject sets that participate in multiple sibling relations so that per-subject sibling attributes are known ground truth.",
"2. Property-collapse error taxonomy: For each model, classify factual errors into (a) property-collapse errors\u2014the wrong answer is a valid attribute of a sibling relation for the same subject (right property, wrong relation), (b) property errors\u2014wrong property value entirely, (c) generic errors. Test whether property-collapse errors are over-represented on high-sibling-overlap relations vs a matched null that controls for answer frequency and entity/relation lexical similarity (logistic regression; chi-square).",
"3. Decoder decomposition: For each relation estimate its LRE (linear relational embedding) from few-shot prompts. Decompose the decoder into a shared-property component (e.g., the low-rank subspace common across sibling LREs, obtained by aligning/averaging sibling operators as in the tensor-network compression) and a relation-specific residual (the operator minus the shared component). Measure how much decoding accuracy each component alone retains.",
"4. Causal transfer via patching: On matched subjects, (a) patch the shared-property component of relation A into a run of sibling relation B and test whether B's answer collapses toward A's answer (should transfer\u2014both share property); (b) patch only the relation-specific residual and test whether it flips the answer to the correct relation-specific attribute (should carry disambiguation). Metric: fraction of cases where each component flips the answer, establishing which component carries the failure.",
"5. Residual-strength predicts collapse: For property-collapse errors vs correct answers on the same relations, measure the magnitude/alignment of the relation-specific residual in the subject representation (via LRE-based projection). Hypothesis: collapse errors have systematically weaker relation-specific residual signal. Metric: AUROC of residual-strength features predicting collapse; label-shuffle control.",
"6. Relation-disambiguation intervention: Learn a low-rank steering vector per sibling group that amplifies the relation-specific residual for the queried relation (computed from mean activation differences between correctly-disambiguated and collapsed matched states). Apply at inference on held-out collapse errors. Metrics: recovery rate, specificity (accuracy on 200 unrelated facts and on sibling relations pre/post to check we don't break siblings), fluency (held-out perplexity), recovered-facts-per-unit-collateral-damage; compare vs ROME/MEMIT edits and a random-direction control.",
"7. Transfer test: Test whether a single disambiguation vector generalizes within a property group (train on one relation pair, apply to held-out sibling pairs) to distinguish a general shared-subspace-sharpening signal from fact-specific fixes.",
"8. Cross-model replication: Repeat core analyses (2\u20136) on GPT-J-6B, Llama-3-8B, and Qwen2.5-7B for architecture-generality."
],
"Risk Factors and Limitations": [
"Property collapse may be rare relative to generic errors, making the effect small; the frequency/similarity-matched baseline is essential and a modest but significant effect would still be informative.",
"LRE decoders are only linear for ~half of relations (Hernandez et al.); analysis is restricted to linearly-decodable relation groups, limiting coverage, which we report explicitly.",
"The shared/residual decomposition is a modeling choice; if the shared and residual components are entangled, the causal patching test (Exp 4) rather than the decomposition itself is the decisive evidence.",
"Wrong answers matching a sibling attribute could arise from generic co-occurrence rather than true property collapse; per-subject ground-truth sibling attributes and residual-strength causal tests mitigate but may not fully eliminate this confound.",
"Disambiguation steering could corrupt sibling-relation recall (over-sharpening); we explicitly measure sibling accuracy pre/post as a specificity control, and utility may be capped even if the analysis holds.",
"A single transferable disambiguation vector may not exist if collapse is relation-pair-specific, limiting practical generality though still scientifically informative.",
"Multi-token attributes complicate LRE readout; primary analysis restricts to single-token-first attributes with reported coverage.",
"Findings on 6\u20138B models may not transfer to frontier-scale models beyond an academic budget."
]
}}
