Action: FinalizeIdea
Arguments: {"idea": {
"Name": "premature_lexicalization",
"Title": "Right Concept, Wrong Word: Premature Lexical Commitment as a Distinct Knowledge-Retrieval Failure in LLMs",
"Short Hypothesis": "When answering factual queries, LLMs internally select the correct target entity as an abstract, token-agnostic concept representation, but the final answer is produced through a separate, later lexicalization step that maps concept to first output token. We hypothesize a distinct failure mode\u2014premature lexicalization\u2014in which the model commits to a high-prior or low-perplexity first token before the concept representation is fully resolved, causing it to emit a lexically-adjacent wrong entity (one sharing the committed first token/subword) even though the correct concept was internally selected. We predict (1) the concept selection and lexical commitment are dissociable and decodable at different layers, (2) a substantial fraction of factual errors are 'right-concept-wrong-word' cases where the correct entity is decodable as a concept but is out-competed at the lexicalization stage by a first-token-sharing distractor, and (3) delaying/re-weighting the lexical commitment (suppressing the prematurely-committed first token direction) recovers the correct entity more selectively than weight editing.",
"Related Work": "A large body of work studies layer-wise commitment: 'Guess-then-Refine' (2025) shows early layers propose high-frequency tokens later refined; Iterative Inference / commitment-depth studies (2024\u20132026) track when models lock in answers; 'How LLMs Use Their Depth' (2025) shows first tokens of multi-token facts need more depth. Geva et al. (2023) describe attribute extraction via attention over an enriched subject. Concept-vs-language separation has been studied for multilingual models (language-agnostic concept spaces) but not for the concept-vs-lexeme distinction within a single language for factual recall. Confidence Regulation / entropy neurons (2024) and token-frequency effects on recall are documented. None of these identify premature lexicalization\u2014a specific dissociation between token-agnostic concept selection and first-token lexical commitment\u2014as a concrete, predictable factual-error mode, nor propose suppressing a prematurely-committed first-token direction as a non-destructive fix. This is orthogonal to my prior proposals (expression gating, format-bound copies, candidate competition among entities, route arbitration, pre-retrieval familiarity, property collapse): here the failure is not choosing the wrong entity but choosing the wrong *word* for a correctly-chosen entity, driven by first-token statistics.",
"Abstract": "Large language models sometimes produce a factual answer that is close to but not the correct entity\u2014e.g., a name that shares a first syllable or subword with the right one. We propose that some of these errors reflect a distinct, overlooked mechanism: premature lexicalization. Under this view, factual recall proceeds in two stages\u2014selecting the target entity as a token-agnostic concept, then lexicalizing that concept into output tokens\u2014and errors arise when the model commits to a high-prior first token before the concept is fully resolved, emitting a lexically-adjacent wrong entity while the correct concept was internally selected. We test three claims. (1) Dissociability: a concept probe (predicting the correct entity in a token-agnostic representation, e.g., via aliases/paraphrastic descriptions) and a lexical probe (predicting the committed first output token) are separable and become decodable at different layers, with concept selection preceding or coexisting with a later lexical-commitment surge. (2) Prevalence: 'right-concept-wrong-word' errors\u2014where the correct entity is decodable as a concept yet the output is a first-token-sharing distractor\u2014form an identifiable fraction of factual errors, concentrated where the correct entity's first token is high-perplexity/rare and a distractor shares a common first token, above frequency-matched baselines. (3) Intervention: suppressing the prematurely-committed distractor first-token direction (or re-scoring the first token conditioned on the concept representation) recovers correct entities with higher specificity and less collateral damage than ROME editing. Experiments span GPT-J-6B, Llama-3-8B, and Qwen2.5-7B on PopQA, T-REx, and EntityQuestions, evaluating probe separability, layer-onset ordering, error-type prevalence, recovery rate, specificity, and fluency.",
"Experiments": [
"1. Concept vs lexical probe construction: On PopQA/T-REx/EntityQuestions, for each factual query train two linear probes on the last-token residual stream at every layer. Concept probe target: entity identity in a token-agnostic space\u2014operationalized by requiring the probe to predict a shared concept ID that is invariant across the entity's aliases/paraphrastic descriptions (train on one surface form, test on held-out aliases). Lexical probe target: the specific first output token the model will emit. Metrics: per-layer accuracy/AUROC for each probe; onset-layer difference (does concept become decodable before the lexical-commitment surge?) with bootstrap CIs; label-shuffle and alias-shuffle controls.",
"2. Orthogonality check: Measure linear predictability between concept and lexical directions; residualize each on the other to confirm non-redundant information (concept should predict correct entity even after removing first-token-predictive variance).",
"3. Right-concept-wrong-word error taxonomy: Classify factual errors into (a) premature-lexicalization errors\u2014the concept probe recovers the correct entity but the emitted answer is a distractor that shares the committed first token/subword with the model's early lexical guess; (b) genuine wrong-concept errors\u2014concept probe recovers the wrong entity; (c) other. Test whether type (a) is over-represented when the correct entity's first token is rare/high-perplexity and a competing entity shares a common first token, versus a null controlling for entity frequency and overall lexical similarity (logistic regression; chi-square).",
"4. Causal test of the two stages via patching: (a) Patch the concept representation from a correct run into a premature-lexicalization failure run and test whether the output switches to the correct entity\u2014this should NOT fix it if the failure is at lexicalization, distinguishing it from wrong-concept errors. (b) Suppress the distractor's first-token unembedding-aligned direction at the lexical-commitment layers and test recovery. (c) Amplify the concept direction alone. These dissociate whether the failure lives in concept selection or in lexicalization.",
"5. Lexical-commitment layer identification: Using logit-lens/tuned-lens, locate the layer where the eventual (wrong) first token overtakes competitors, and compare to the layer where the concept probe first recovers the correct entity. Hypothesis: for premature-lexicalization errors, concept-correct-layer <= lexical-commitment-layer, and the committed token has an anomalously early/high early-layer prior (high frequency) relative to correct-answer cases.",
"6. Recovery intervention: (a) Suppress the prematurely-committed distractor first-token direction at the commitment layers; (b) re-score the first token by projecting the concept representation to constrain the candidate set (a lightweight concept-conditioned first-token re-ranking); compare against ROME/MEMIT edits and a random-direction control. Metrics: recovery rate, specificity (accuracy on 200 unrelated facts pre/post), fluency (held-out perplexity), recovered-facts-per-unit-collateral-damage.",
"7. Transfer and generality: Test whether a single suppression/re-ranking recipe generalizes across relations (train identification on one relation subset, apply to held-out relations); repeat core analyses (1,3,4,6) on GPT-J-6B, Llama-3-8B, and Qwen2.5-7B for architecture-generality.",
"8. Source analysis: Regress premature-lexicalization error rate on first-token frequency of the correct answer, first-token overlap with high-frequency distractors, and number of tokens in the correct answer, to quantify how much the failure is driven by first-token statistics (the core mechanistic claim)."
],
"Risk Factors and Limitations": [
"Defining a truly token-agnostic 'concept' probe is hard; alias-based invariance is imperfect and may leak surface information. We use held-out aliases and paraphrastic descriptions and report coverage; the causal patching test (Exp 4) is the decisive evidence rather than probe onset alone.",
"Premature-lexicalization errors may be rare relative to genuine wrong-concept errors, yielding a small effect; the frequency/similarity-matched baseline is essential and a modest significant effect remains informative.",
"Concept and lexical stages may be entangled (not cleanly two-stage), weakening the strong dissociation claim; graded results would still be scientifically informative and we report continuous measures.",
"Suppressing a first-token direction could degrade fluency or introduce new errors when the direction overlaps useful features; strict specificity and perplexity controls are included and utility may be capped even if the analysis holds.",
"Analysis focuses on entities where first-token distinctions are meaningful; single-token answers and heavily subword-fragmented names complicate readout, and we report coverage restrictions.",
"A single transferable intervention recipe may not exist if the failure is fact-specific, limiting practical generality though still scientifically informative.",
"Findings on 6\u20138B models may not transfer to frontier-scale models beyond an academic budget."
]
}}
