s a common first token, above frequency-matched baselines. (3) Intervention: suppressing the prematurely-committed distractor first-token direction (or re-scoring the first token conditioned on the concept representation) recovers correct entities with higher specificity and less collateral damage than ROME editing. Experiments span GPT-J-6B, Llama-3-8B, and Qwen2.5-7B on PopQA, T-REx, and EntityQuestions, evaluating probe separability, layer-onset ordering, error-type prevalence, recovery rate, specificity, and fluency.", 'Experiments': ["1. Concept vs lexical probe construction: On PopQA/T-REx/EntityQuestions, for each factual query train two linear probes on the last-token residual stream at every layer. Concept probe target: entity identity in a token-agnostic space—operationalized by requiring the probe to predict a shared concept ID that is invariant across the entity's aliases/paraphrastic descriptions (train on one surface form, test on held-out aliases). Lexical probe target: the specific first output token the model will emit. Metrics: per-layer accuracy/AUROC for each probe; onset-layer difference (does concept become decodable before the lexical-commitment surge?) with bootstrap CIs; label-shuffle and alias-shuffle controls.", '2. Orthogonality check: Measure linear predictability between concept and lexical directions; residualize each on the other to confirm non-redundant information (concept should predict correct entity even after removing first-token-predictive variance).', "3. Right-concept-wrong-word error taxonomy: Classify factual errors into (a) premature-lexicalization errors—the concept probe recovers the correct entity but the emitted answer is a distractor that shares the committed first token/subword with the model's early lexical guess; (b) genuine wrong-concept errors—concept probe recovers the wrong entity; (c) other. Test whether type (a) is over-represented when the correct entity's first token is rare/high-perplexity and a competing entity shares a common first token, versus a null controlling for entity frequency and overall lexical similarity (logistic regression; chi-square).", "4. Causal test of the two stages via patching: (a) Patch the concept representation from a correct run into a premature-lexicalization failure run and test whether the output switches to the correct entity—this should NOT fix it if the failure is at lexicalization, distinguishing it from wrong-concept errors. (b) Suppress the distractor's first-token unembedding-aligned direction at the lexical-commitment layers and test recovery. (c) Amplify the concept direction alone. These dissociate whether the failure lives in concept selection or in lexicalization.", '5. Lexical-commitment layer identification: Using logit-lens/tuned-lens, locate the layer where the eventual (wrong) first token overtakes competitors, and compare to the layer where the concept probe first recovers the correct entity. Hypothesis: for premature-lexicalization errors, concept-correct-layer <= lexical-commitment-layer, and the committed token has an anomalously early/high early-layer prior (high frequency) relative to correct-answer cases.', '6. Recovery intervention: (a) Suppress the prematurely-committed distractor first-token direction at the commitment layers; (b) re-score the first token by projecting the concept representation to constrain the candidate set (a lightweight concept-conditioned first-token re-ranking); compare against ROME/MEMIT edits and a random-direction control. Metrics: recovery rate, specificity (accuracy on 200 unrelated facts pre/post), fluency (held-out perplexity), recovered-facts-per-unit-collateral-damage.', '7. Transfer and generality: Test whether a single suppression/re-ranking recipe generalizes across relations (train identification on one relation subset, apply to held-out relations); repeat core analyses (1,3,4,6) on GPT-J-6B, Llama-3-8B, and Qwen2.5-7B for architecture-generality.', '8. Source analysis: Regress premature-lexicalization error rate on first-token frequency of the correct answer, first-token overlap with high-frequency distractors, and number of tokens in the correct answer, to quantify how much the failure is driven by first-token statistics (the core mechanistic claim).'], 'Risk Factors and Limitations': ["Defining a truly token-agnostic 'concept' probe is hard; alias-based invariance is imperfect and may leak surface information. We use held-out aliases and paraphrastic descriptions and report coverage; the causal patching test (Exp 4) is the decisive evidence rather than probe onset alone.", 'Premature-lexicalization errors may be rare relative to genuine wrong-concept errors, yielding a small effect; the frequency/similarity-matched baseline is essential and a modest significant effect remains informative.', 'Concept and lexical stages may be entangled (not cleanly two-stage), weakening the strong dissociation claim; graded results would still be scientifically informative and we report continuous measures.', 'Suppressing a first-token direction could degrade fluency or introduce new errors when the direction overlaps useful features; strict specificity and perplexity controls are included and utility may be capped even if the analysis holds.', 'Analysis focuses on entities where first-token distinctions are meaningful; single-token answers and heavily subword-fragmented names complicate readout, and we report coverage restrictions.', 'A single transferable intervention recipe may not exist if the failure is fact-specific, limiting practical generality though still scientifically informative.', 'Findings on 6–8B models may not transfer to frontier-scale models beyond an academic budget.']}

