Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "schema_overregularization", "Title": "Overregularization in LLMs: When a Stored Exception Loses to a Generalizable Rule It Contradicts", "Short Hypothesis": "LLMs store both broad generalizable rules/schemata (e.g., 'the plural of X is X+s', 'a country's capital is its largest city', 'company founders are CEOs') and specific exception facts that violate those rules. We hypothesize a distinct, predictable failure mode\u2014overregularization\u2014in which the model retrieves and produces the rule-consistent answer even when it internally stores the correct exception, because the rule pathway and the exception pathway compete and the rule wins on ambiguous or low-frequency exceptions. We predict (1) rule application and exception recall are causally dissociable pathways decodable at different depths, (2) overregularization errors concentrate on low-frequency exceptions where the rule is strong, above matched baselines, and (3) suppressing the rule pathway (not amplifying the fact) selectively recovers the stored exception with high specificity, whereas ROME-style fact editing does not address the root cause.", "Related Work": "Prior work shows reasoning/generalization and memorization occupy separable-but-interacting circuits and layers (Disentangling Recall and Reasoning, 2025; Decoupling Knowledge and Reasoning, 2025; Demystifying LLM Layers, 2025), that memorization builds on and coexists with generalization mechanisms with intermediate reasoning outputs computed even when a memorized label is emitted (Reason to Rote, 2025), and that a single linear direction mediates a reasoning-vs-memorization switch (LiReFs, 2025). Declarative statements causally shape generalization (Tell, don't show, 2023). All of these characterize that rule/generalization and fact/memorization mechanisms co-exist, but none identify overregularization\u2014the rule pathway overriding a correctly-stored exception\u2014as a concrete, predictable factual-answering failure, none causally localize the failure to rule-vs-exception competition, and none propose rule-pathway suppression as a non-destructive fix. This is also orthogonal to my prior proposals (expression gating, format-bound copies, entity-candidate competition, retrieve-vs-assemble route arbitration, pre-retrieval familiarity, property collapse, premature lexicalization): here the competition is between a productive rule and a fact that violates it, mirroring the classic human overregularization phenomenon (e.g., 'goed' for 'went').", "Abstract": "Large language models generalize from productive rules and schemata\u2014regular plurals, default relational patterns, statistical regularities\u2014while also storing specific facts that violate those rules. We propose that this dual organization creates a distinct, overlooked failure mode we call overregularization: when a query targets an exception, the model produces the rule-consistent answer even though it internally stores the correct exception, because a rule-application pathway competes with and overrides an exception-recall pathway. Under this account, some knowledge failures are neither storage nor gating failures but rule-vs-exception arbitration failures, directly paralleling human overregularization ('goed' for 'went'). We build controlled rule/exception item sets across three domains\u2014morphology (irregular inflection), factual relations with statistical defaults (e.g., capital\u2260largest-city, founder\u2260CEO), and numeric/temporal regularities\u2014and identify exceptions that the model stores (recoverable under strong prompting or by a linear content probe) yet answers with the rule-consistent value. We test three claims. (1) Dissociability: a rule-strength probe (predicting the rule-consistent output) and an exception-content probe (predicting the correct stored exception) are separable and decodable at different layers, with rule signals emerging early. (2) Prevalence: overregularization errors concentrate on low-frequency exceptions with high rule support, above frequency- and similarity-matched baselines, and the wrong answers are disproportionately the exact rule-predicted value. (3) Intervention: suppressing the rule pathway (via a learned low-rank rule direction) recovers the stored exception with higher specificity and less collateral damage than amplifying the fact or ROME editing, and preserves correct rule application on true regulars. Experiments span GPT-J-6B, Llama-3-8B, and Qwen2.5-7B, evaluating probe separability, layer-onset ordering, error-type prevalence, causal transfer, recovery rate, specificity, and fluency.", "Experiments": ["1. Rule/exception item construction: Build controlled datasets in three domains. (a) Morphology: regular vs irregular inflections (plurals, past tense) where the rule (add -s/-ed) is productive and irregulars are exceptions. (b) Factual relations with statistical defaults: relations whose default answer is usually true but has documented exceptions\u2014capital-vs-largest-city, founder-vs-CEO, author-vs-most-famous-work-year\u2014with per-item labels for rule-predicted value and the true exception value. (c) Numeric/temporal regularities (e.g., '30 days hath...' month-length exceptions). For each item, compute the rule-predicted answer and the correct answer, and flag exceptions (rule-predicted \u2260 correct).", "2. Storage verification for exceptions: For each exception, confirm the model actually stores it via (i) strong retrieval prompts / few-shot demonstrations and (ii) a linear content probe on the residual stream decoding the correct answer. Only exceptions with positive storage evidence enter the 'stored-exception' set; report coverage.", "3. Dual-probe construction and layer-onset test: Train two linear probes at every layer on the last-token residual stream: a rule-strength probe (target: the rule-predicted output token / a scalar rule-applicability score) and an exception-content probe (target: the correct stored answer). Metrics: per-layer AUROC/accuracy for each probe; test whether the rule signal becomes decodable at earlier layers than the exception content (onset-layer difference with bootstrap CIs); label-shuffle controls. Orthogonality check via residualization to confirm non-redundant information.", "4. Overregularization error taxonomy and prevalence: Classify errors on exception queries into (a) overregularization\u2014the emitted answer equals the exact rule-predicted value while the exception is internally stored (per Exp 2); (b) other errors; (c) correct. Test whether overregularization concentrates on low-frequency exceptions with high rule support, using logistic regression / chi-square against a null controlling for exception-answer frequency and lexical similarity to the rule-predicted answer.", "5. Causal decomposition via patching: (a) Patch the exception-content representation from a correct run into an overregularization-failure run and test whether the output switches to the exception. (b) Suppress the rule direction (identified in Exp 3) at the rule-onset layers and test recovery of the exception. (c) Amplify the exception-content direction alone. These dissociate whether the failure lives in weak exception recall or in an overriding rule pathway, and whether rule suppression (not fact amplification) is the effective lever. As a positive control, verify that rule suppression on true regulars degrades their (correct) rule-based answers, confirming we manipulated the rule pathway.", "6. Rule-suppression recovery intervention: Learn a low-rank rule steering vector (mean activation difference between rule-dominant and exception-dominant matched states) and subtract it on held-out overregularization failures. Metrics: exception recovery rate; specificity\u2014accuracy on (i) 200 unrelated facts and (ii) a held-out set of true regulars (which must remain correct) pre/post; fluency (held-out perplexity); recovered-exceptions-per-unit-collateral-damage. Compare against exception-direction amplification, ROME/MEMIT editing of the exception, and a random-direction control.", "7. Transfer and generality: Test whether a single rule-suppression vector generalizes across items within a rule family and across rule families (train on one domain/rule, apply to held-out ones); repeat core analyses (2\u20136) on GPT-J-6B, Llama-3-8B, and Qwen2.5-7B for architecture-generality.", "8. Source analysis: Regress overregularization rate on exception-answer frequency, rule productivity (fraction of the family that follows the rule), and the frequency ratio between rule-predicted and true answers, to quantify how much the failure is driven by rule strength versus exception weakness (the core mechanistic claim)."], "Risk Factors and Limitations": ["Cleanly separating 'rule' from 'exception' representations may be hard when they are entangled (superposition); the causal patching and rule-suppression tests (Exp 5\u20136), rather than probe onset alone, are the decisive evidence, and graded results would still be informative.", "Overregularization errors may be rare relative to generic errors; the frequency/similarity-matched baseline is essential and a modest but significant, well-localized effect remains publishable.", "Establishing that an exception is truly 'stored' risks overestimation from strong-prompt leakage; we use conservative content-probe thresholds and held-out verification and report coverage limits.", "Rule suppression could break correct rule application on genuine regulars or degrade fluency; the true-regulars specificity control (Exp 5c/6) is central and utility may be capped even if the analysis holds.", "A single transferable rule-suppression vector may not exist if rules are family-specific, limiting practical generality though still scientifically informative.", "Multi-token answers complicate probe/logit-lens readout; primary analysis restricts to single-token-first answers with reported coverage.", "Findings on 6\u20138B models may not transfer to frontier-scale models beyond an academic budget."]}}
Proposal finalized: {'Name': 'schema_overregularization', 'Title': 'Overregularization in LLMs: When a Stored Exception Loses to a Generalizable Rule It Contradicts', 'Short Hypothesis': "LLMs store both broad generalizable rules/schemata (e.g., 'the plural of X is X+s', 'a country's capital is its largest city', 'company founders are CEOs') and specific exception facts that violate those rules. We hypothesize a distinct, predictable failure mode—overregularization—in which the model retrieves and produces the rule-consistent answer even when it internally stores the correct exception, because the rule pathway and the exception pathway compete and the rule wins on ambiguous or low-frequency exceptions. We predict (1) rule application and exception recall are causally dissociable pathways decodable at different depths, (2) overregularization errors concentrate on low-frequency exceptions where the rule is strong, above matched baselines, and (3) suppressing the rule pathway (not amplifying the fact) selectively recovers the stored exception with high specificity, whereas ROME-style fact editing does not address the root cause.", 'Related Work': "Prior work shows reasoning/generalization and memorization occupy separable-but-interacting circuits and layers (Disentangling Recall and Reasoning, 2025; Decoupling Knowledge and Reasoning, 2025; Demystifying LLM Layers, 2025), that memorization builds on and coexists with generalization mechanisms with intermediate reasoning outputs computed even when a memorized label is emitted (Reason to Rote, 2025), and that a single linear direction mediates a reasoning-vs-memorization switch (LiReFs, 2025). Declarative statements causally shape generalization (Tell, don't show, 2023). All of these characterize that rule/generalization and fact/memorization mechanisms co-exist, but none identify overregularization—the rule pathway overriding a correctly-stored exception—as a concrete, predictable factual-answering failure, none causally localize the failure to rule-vs-exception competition, and none propose rule-pathway suppression as a non-destructive fix. This is also orthogonal to my prior proposals (expression gating, format-bound copies, entity-candidate competition, retrieve-vs-assemble route arbitration, pre-retrieval familiarity, property collapse, premature lexicalization): here the competition is between a productive rule and a fact that violates it, mirroring the classic human overregularization phenomenon (e.g., 'goed' for 'went').", 'Abstract': "Large language models generalize from productive rules and schemata—regular plurals, default relational patterns, statistical regularities—while also storing specific facts that violate those rules. We propose that this dual organization creates a distinct, overlooked failure mode we call overregularization: when a query targets an exception, the model produces the rule-consistent answer even though it internally stores the correct exception, because a rule-application pathway competes with and overrides an exception-recall pathway. Under this account, some knowledge failures are neither storage nor gating failures but rule-vs-exception arbitration failures, directly paralleling human overregularization ('goed' for 'went'). We build controlled rule/exception item sets across three domains—morphology (irregular inflection), factual relations with statistical defaults (e.g., capital≠largest-city, founder≠CEO), and numeric/temporal regularities—and identify exceptions that the model stores (recoverable under strong prompting or by a linear content probe) yet answers with the rule-consistent value. We test three claims. (1) Dissociability: a rule-strength probe (predicting the rule-consistent output) and an exception-content probe (predicting the correct stored exception) are separable and decodable at different layers, with rule signals emerging early. (2) Prevalence: overregularization errors concentrate on low-frequency exceptions with high rule support, above frequency- and similarity-matched baselines, and the wrong answers are disproportionately the exact rule-predicted value. (3) Intervention: suppressing the rule pathway (via a learned low-rank rule direction) recovers the stored exception with higher specificity and less collateral damage than amplifying the fact or ROME editing, and preserves correct rule application on true regulars. Experiments span GPT-J-6B, Llama-3-8B, and Qwen2.5-7B, evaluating probe separability, layer-onset ordering, error-type prevalence, causal transfer, recovery rate, specificity, and fluency.", 'Experiments': ["1. Rule/exception item construction: Build controlled datasets in three domains. (a) Morphology: regular vs irregular inflections (plurals, past tense) where the rule (add -s/-ed) is productive and irregulars are exceptions. (b) Factual relations with statistical defaults: relations whose default answer is usually true but has documented exceptions—capital-vs-largest-city, founder-vs-CEO, author-vs-most-famous-work-year—with per-item labels for rule-predicted value and the true exception value. (c) Numeric/temporal regularities (e.g., '30 days hath...' month-length exceptions). For each item, compute the rule-predicted answer and the correct answer, and flag exceptions (rule-predicted ≠ correct).", "2. Storage verification for exceptions: For each exception, confirm the model actually stores it via (i) strong retrieval prompts / few-shot demonstrations and (ii) a linear content probe on the residual stream decoding the correct answer. Only exceptions with positive storage evidence enter the 'stored-exception' set; report coverage.", '3. Dual-probe construction and layer-onset test: Train two linear probes at every layer on the last-token residual stream: a rule-strength probe (target: the rule-predicted output token / a scalar rule-applicability score) and an exception-content probe (target: the correct stored answer). Metrics: per-layer AUROC/accuracy for each probe; test whether the rule signal becomes decodable at earlier layers than the exception content (onset-layer difference with bootstrap CIs); label-shuffle controls. Orthogonality check via residualization to confirm non-redundant information.', '4. Overregularization error taxonomy and prevalence: Classify errors on exception queries into (a) overregularization—the emitted answer equals the exact rule-predicted value while the exception is internally stored (per Exp 2); (b) other errors; (c) correct. Test whether overregularization concentrates on low-frequency exceptions with high rule support, using logistic regression / chi-square against a null controlling for exception-answer frequency and lexical similarity to the rule-predicted answer.', '5. Causal decomposition via patching: (a) Patch the exception-content representation from a correct run into an overregularization-failure run and test whether the output switches to the exception. (b) Suppress the rule direction (identified in Exp 3) at the rule-onset layers and test recovery of the exception. (c) Amplify the exception-content direction alone. These dissociate whether the failure lives in weak exception recall or in an overriding rule pathway, and whether rule suppression (not fact amplification) is the effective lever. As a positive control, verify that rule suppression on true regulars degrades their (correct) rule-based answers, confirming we manipulated the rule pathway.', '6. Rule-suppression recovery intervention: Learn a low-rank rule steering vector (mean activation difference between rule-dominant and exception-dominant matched states) and subtract it on held-out overregularization failures. Metrics: exception recovery rate; specificity—accuracy on (i) 200 unrelated facts and (ii) a held-out set of true regulars (which must remain correct) pre/post; fluency (held-out perplexity); recovered-exceptions-per-unit-collateral-damage. Compare against exception-direction amplification, ROME/MEMIT editing of the exception, and a random-direction control.', '7. Transfer and generality: Test whether a single rule-suppression vector generalizes across items within a rule family and across rule families (train on one domain/rule, apply to held-out ones); repeat core analyses (2–6) on GPT-J-6B, Llama-3-8B, and Qwen2.5-7B for architecture-generality.', '8. Source analysis: Regress overregularization rate on exception-answer frequency, rule productivity (fraction of the family that follows the rule), and the frequency ratio between rule-predicted and true answers, to quantify how much the failure is driven by rule strength versus exception weakness (the core mechanistic claim).'], 'Risk Factors and Limitations': ["Cleanly separating 'rule' from 'exception' representations may be hard when they are entangled (superposition); the causal patching and rule-suppression tests (Exp 5–6), rather than probe onset alone, are the decisive evidence, and graded results would still be informative.", 'Overregularization errors may be rare relative to generic errors; the frequency/similarity-matched baseline is essential and a modest but significant, well-localized effect remains publishable.', "Establishing that an exception is truly 'stored' risks overestimation from strong-prompt leakage; we use conservative content-probe thresholds and held-out verification and report coverage limits.", 'Rule suppression could break correct rule application on genuine regulars or degrade fluency; the true-regulars specificity control (Exp 5c/6) is central and utility may be capped even if the analysis holds.', 'A single transferable rule-suppression vector may not exist if rules are family-specific, limiting practical generality though still scientifically informative.', 'Multi-token answers complicate probe/logit-lens readout; primary analysis restricts to single-token-first answers with reported coverage.', 'Findings on 6–8B models may not transfer to frontier-scale models beyond an academic budget.']}

