in a large language model should ripple to its logical consequences (edit 'X's employer to Google' and 'X's boss' should follow), but existing methods propagate updates inconsistently. Prior explanations tie ripple failure to the edit itself — e.g., gradient similarity between the edited fact and its consequence. We propose that ripple success is largely determined *before any edit* by the geometry of how the model already stores the two facts. When the model is internally consistent about a logically-linked pair, their subject/relation representations occupy a shared, aligned subspace so an update to one is naturally read out by the other; when they are stored in misaligned, disconnected subspaces — a 'consistency seam' — updates cannot propagate. We introduce a static, edit-free coupling measure: for a fact pair (A, B) where B is entailed by A, we quantify the alignment between the subspaces that carry A's and B's answer-relevant information (via linear relational embeddings and CCA/subspace-angle between the residual-stream directions that read out each answer). We test three claims. (1) Predictiveness: pre-edit coupling predicts per-pair ripple success at edit time (ROME/MEMIT/in-context) with higher AUROC than GradSim, and does so without computing any edit. (2) Causality: ripple failures concentrate on low-coupling 'seam' pairs above frequency- and hop-matched baselines; injecting the missing coupling (aligning the subspaces) improves ripple. (3) Native inconsistency: even unedited, seam pairs already exhibit belief inconsistency (the model affirms A and B's rule but denies the entailed answer) more than coupled pairs. Experiments span GPT-J-6B, Llama-3-8B, and Qwen2.5-7B on RippleEdits and MQuAKE, evaluating AUROC of ripple prediction, seam prevalence, causal recovery, and specificity.", 'Experiments': ["1. Fact-pair and coupling construction: From RippleEdits and MQuAKE, extract pairs (A, B) where B is a logical/compositional consequence of A (two-hop, sibling, inverse relations). For each fact, estimate the answer-readout direction/subspace in the residual stream using linear relational embeddings (LRE) and answer-token logit-lens/tuned-lens probes at the subject and last positions. Define the pre-edit coupling score as the alignment between A's and B's readout subspaces — principal subspace angles / CCA correlation between the directions that carry each fact's answer information — computed with NO reference to any edit or target answer.", '2. Edit-free ripple prediction: Independently apply three editing methods (ROME, MEMIT, in-context editing) to A and measure per-pair ripple success on B. Test whether the pre-edit coupling score predicts ripple success across pairs and across methods (AUROC, per-method and pooled). Baseline comparison: GradSim (requires the edit) and simple controls (entity/answer frequency, hop count, lexical overlap). Key result target: coupling matches or beats GradSim while requiring no edit-specific gradient.', "3. Seam prevalence and geometry: Classify pairs into 'coupled' (high alignment) and 'seam' (low alignment) via a cross-validated threshold. Test whether ripple failures concentrate on seam pairs above frequency-/hop-matched null baselines (logistic regression; chi-square). Characterize what predicts seams: relation type, entity popularity asymmetry between A and B, and whether A and B were likely co-present in text (co-occurrence proxy on an open-data model, Pythia, where feasible).", "4. Native (pre-edit) inconsistency: Without editing, probe whether the model already holds inconsistent beliefs on seam pairs — it correctly answers A, affirms the connecting rule (e.g., 'the spouse of the PM is the PM's partner'), yet fails/denies the entailed fact B — at higher rates than coupled pairs. Metric: inconsistency rate difference between seam and coupled groups, establishing that seams reflect pre-existing organizational fractures, not just edit artifacts.", "5. Causal test via subspace alignment: For seam pairs, learn a low-rank alignment map that rotates B's readout subspace toward A's (estimated from coupled pairs of the same relation family), inject it, then edit A and re-measure ripple to B. Compare ripple recovery against (a) no alignment, (b) ChainEdit-style logical-chain in-context prompting, and (c) a random-rotation control. Metrics: ripple recovery rate; specificity (unrelated-fact accuracy and unrelated-pair ripple pre/post); fluency (held-out perplexity). This tests whether restoring coupling causally enables propagation.", '6. Transfer and generality: Test whether coupling scores computed on one relation family predict ripple on held-out families, and repeat core analyses (1–4) on GPT-J-6B, Llama-3-8B, and Qwen2.5-7B for architecture-generality. Report whether larger models have fewer seams (consistent with prior findings that larger models ripple better).'], 'Risk Factors and Limitations': ['The coupling/subspace-alignment measure is a modeling choice; LREs are linear for only ~half of relations, limiting coverage — we report which relations admit reliable readout directions and restrict primary analysis accordingly.', "Coupling may correlate with simple confounds (co-occurrence, popularity, hop count); matched baselines and multivariate regression are essential, and the edit-free predictor's value rests on beating GradSim after controls.", "Predicting ripple 'before any edit' could still leak information if the readout subspaces implicitly encode edit-relevant structure; we mitigate by computing coupling with no target answer and validating on held-out relation families.", 'The causal subspace-alignment intervention may fail if coupling is not a single low-rank map or if aligning subspaces damages unrelated readouts; strict specificity/fluency controls are included and the causal claim may hold only partially even if prediction succeeds.', "Native-inconsistency measurement depends on eliciting the model's 'belief' about the rule, which is prompt-sensitive; we use multiple phrasings and report robustness.", 'Multi-token answers complicate readout-direction estimation; primary analysis restricts to single-token-first answers with reported coverage.', 'Co-occurrence origin analysis is only feasible on small open-data models (Pythia) and may not transfer to the 6–8B evaluation models.', 'Findings on 6–8B models may not transfer to frontier-scale models beyond an academic budget.']}
